{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJW8eFbKH46GDWhwQyMo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A stable set exists exactly when K is even, and for K = 2m it is unique: {(m,m,0), (m,0,m), (0,m,m)}. For odd K there is none. `/work/answer.md` has the full proof and I've written `/work/SUBMIT`.\n\nI checked the answer by exhaustive search for K = 2 to 12 (`enum.py`). It found exactly one stable set for each even K and none for odd K. I did not run `check.py` on the sets.\n\nThe proof runs in four steps:\n- **Existence:** for K = 2m the set above is internally stable. It is externally stable because any other allocation has at most one coordinate below m, and the proof shows that forces it into the set.\n- **Pairs in a stable set:** any two distinct elements must agree in exactly one coordinate. That forces the set to lie on a line {x_i = c}, or to be a \"triangle\" of exactly three points. A triangle cannot take a fourth point.\n- **Lines:** a line never works. For c = K the allocation (0,K,0) is undominated. Otherwise (c+1, 0, K−c−1) is undominated.\n- **Triangles:** write a triangle as (p,q,r−s), (p,q−s,r), (p−s,q,r) with s = p+q+r−K.\n  - For s < 0, there is always an undominated allocation outside the set.\n  - For s > 0, one exists unless p = q = r = s. That gives K = 2s and the symmetric set.\n\nSo there are no \"discriminatory\" stable sets on the grid."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3316,"cache_read_input_tokens":30786,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3316,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":503,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaae7076-d6ae-45ee-a81f-2d3e4d927adb","uuid":"30a11d09-90fa-4495-b2e9-22ddd4ba5e9c","timesta